{"kind":"artifact","text":"The browser asks the fold's own membership question before offering a ratification, and shows every citation before it is signed. Exact clean pushed head f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c on request/decision-records-ui-recut; local and origin identical by commit SHA and tree hash 340806ed, checkout clean. 13 files, +1490/-32.\n\nTHE SECURITY REPAIR, which is the reason this lane exists. authority.ts stopped treating presence in Projection.Actors as proof of membership. The fold deliberately keeps a departed principal in that map with retired=true and no roles, because their signatures are permanent; decideRatify tests the participant role instead. So the old reading offered \"accept\" to someone the fold will refuse, and pressing it appended a permanent ineffective row — the precise outcome this lane was filed to prevent. mayRatify now requires the projected participant role.\n\nI CHECKED THE EQUIVALENCE RATHER THAN ASSERTING IT. internal/workroom/fold.go hasActor is literally a participant-role test. I also checked the asymmetry that would break the claim: the fold consults active role grants, while the browser reads a projected roles array, so the two agree only if the projection populates that array from active grants alone. It does — inactive grants route to DormantRoleSources and RetiredRoleSources instead. mayRatify is fail-closed on every path. The rendered regression covers the departed-author shape, retired=true with no roles and matching requester and viewer fingerprints, and it fails against the unrepaired authority.ts.\n\nWHAT ELSE IS HERE. records.ts and modalFocus.ts carry the citation composer and its focus contract: the composer renders the same bases array it submits, drawn from projected provenance alone, ordered by the fold's own sequence so the result never depends on serialization order, and bounded by a fixed limit so no screen can drive a record past the kernel's causal-reference ceiling. The operator reads the citation before signing and the fold judges it afterwards. The tests are at rendered-output altitude rather than asserting internals.\n\nONE FALSE-OFFER SITE REMAINS AFTER THIS HEAD, AND I AM NAMING IT RATHER THAN WIDENING SCOPE. semanticActions still offers a departed viewer needs-work and withdraw on their own rows, which the fold refuses for want of live participation. It is the same fail-open as mayRatify at a different site. I found it while repairing this one and did not implement it unauthorized; planner filed it as ba9326ae, which I have promised at afdb30c5 and which is sequenced to start only after this head merges. A reviewer should expect to see it still present here.\n\nGATES RUN BY ME AT THIS EXACT HEAD. npm test --prefix ui 97 tests, 97 pass, 0 fail, 0 cancelled; make ui exit 0 leaving git status empty, so the committed embed is byte-reproduced from this source; make ui-check exit 0; go test ./... -count=1 exit 0, 33 packages, zero failures, zero cached; gofmt, build, vet and git diff --check all clean.","body":{"commit":"f6489c78a4e9c11460ebe95e3312f1acd1d0de2c","path":"ui"}}